--- body ---
## Context
The chain mixes two kinds of statement that have different audiences,
different lifetimes and different disclosure risk.

Specification: what the system promises (R-numbers), what the world imposes
(C-numbers), how the design answers (DESIGN.md), and which technical
questions are settled (the D-entries in DECISIONS.md).

Governance: at most two upstream PRs in flight; the contained-PR budget of
six ratified and five live; design-sized asks go as issues the maintainer
builds himself; the three-week sunset on unaddressed items; the acceptance
harness as a one-time transfer; the reading of the maintainer's behaviour
that justifies each of those.

The IETF separates these deliberately — process in BCPs, protocol in RFCs —
and the reason is not tidiness. A reader who wants to implement the system
has no use for the PR cap, and a reader who wants to understand the upstream
relationship should not have to read 7 460 words of architecture to find it.

There is a second reason, specific to this repository. CLAUDE.md already
warns that this repo is public and the maintainer reads it, and that
governance and strategy-about-the-maintainer must never enter upstream
text. Today that separation is enforced by the care of whoever is writing.
A structural split makes it enforceable by a grep instead: strategy lives in
one file, and a check can assert that no other document names it.

## OPEN QUESTION FOR THE AUTHOR — the split
The author asked whether this should be standalone.

(a) Standalone GOVERNANCE.md, with DECISIONS.md keeping only technical
    D-entries. Cleanest separation. Cost: DECISIONS.md is append-only and
    ratified, so moving entries out of it is exactly the edit that document
    forbids.

(b) Standalone GOVERNANCE.md that OWNS the process rules going forward,
    while ratified process entries stay in DECISIONS.md where they are,
    with GOVERNANCE.md pointing at them. Nothing ratified is edited; the
    ledger stays append-only; new process rulings land in the new document.

(c) Leave the text where it is and mark it. A `[process]` tag on process
    entries, and a section index. Cheapest, and it does not solve the
    disclosure problem, since strategy is still interleaved with spec.

Recommendation: (b). It is the only one of the three that respects the
append-only rule, which is not a preference but the property that makes the
ledger trustworthy. The cost is a transitional period where process rulings
live in two places, resolved by the pointer rather than by a move.

Note that SYNC.md already holds much of the maintainer-relationship
material. The question is partly whether GOVERNANCE.md is a new document or
whether SYNC.md is promoted to be it. SYNC.md is upstream *tracking* — it
records what happened. Governance is what we have decided to do about it.
Related but not the same, and SYNC.md is rewritten at each upstream
movement while governance should be stable.

Not to be decided by an agent — the ruling lands in DECISIONS.md first.

## Relevant files
- `DECISIONS.md` — the ratified entries, some technical, some process
- `CLAUDE.md` — the binding upstream-relations paragraph
- `SYNC.md` — upstream tracking, adjacent
- `README.md` — the authoritative-chain sentence

## Actions
1. Author rules on (a), (b) or (c), and on the SYNC.md question. Ratify.
2. Inventory every process and strategy statement in the chain, with its
   current location.
3. Apply the ruling. Under (b), write GOVERNANCE.md as the owner going
   forward and point back at the ratified entries rather than moving them.
4. Update CLAUDE.md and README.md so the split is legible to a newcomer.

## Test
A check that greps the specification documents for the governance
vocabulary (PR cap, sunset, maintainer, upstream strategy) and fails when it
appears outside the governing document and its explicit pointers. Red first:
it must fire on the current tree.

## Verification
- [ ] No ratified DECISIONS.md entry is edited
- [ ] A reader can find every process bound in one place
- [ ] The public-disclosure hazard is enforced by a check, not by care

## Invariants
- DECISIONS.md stays append-only. This is the binding constraint on the
  whole ticket.
- No governance content is weakened or dropped in the move; this is
  relocation, not revision.
- Nothing about upstream strategy enters any text destined for upstream.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The split is ratified in DECISIONS.md
- [ ] Governance statements have one owner, reachable in one hop
- [ ] The grep-level guard is in place and `make check` passes
